WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.815 --> 00:00:07.940
ABHI YADAV: Hello, and
welcome to the next breakout

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:09.440
session in security.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:11.570
In this session, we take
you through a deep dive

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:13.520
in solving a critical
but often hard

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:17.510
to accomplish security
objective of least privilege.

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:18.740
My name is Abhi Yadav.

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:21.290
I'm a product manager at
Google for Identity and Access

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:23.420
Management, and today,
I'm excited to share

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:26.050
with you Google's
latest innovation in IAM

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:28.430
called Policy Intelligence.

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:30.470
And joining me today
is a brilliant panel.

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:32.689
First, we have Vandy
who's a product manager

00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:35.830
and researcher at Google
working in Policy Intelligence.

00:00:35.830 --> 00:00:38.033
Hi, Vandy.

00:00:38.033 --> 00:00:39.200
VANDHANA RAMADURA: Hi, Abhi.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:41.968
Hi, everyone.

00:00:41.968 --> 00:00:44.260
ABHI YADAV: And later joining
us is Guillaume Blaquiere

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:46.420
who's a Certified
Expert in Google Cloud

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:48.850
and also the lead
architect of Veolia,

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:53.060
an organization with more
than 170,000 employees.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:55.330
And he will walk us through
some of Veolia's advanced

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:59.830
tooling and journey in achieving
least privilege in the Cloud.

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:02.350
Now, when I think of
the Cloud, I sometimes

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:05.000
think of this airplane cockpit.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.560
But the Cloud probably
has more knobs and dials

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:10.420
than this cockpit does.

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:12.170
It is rather complicated.

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:14.890
So some customers, like
Veolia, have tens of thousands

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:17.260
of projects in the Cloud,
and these resources

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:19.930
get spun up and down faster than
the security teams can keep up

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:20.850
with them.

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:23.620
Add to this multiple
layers of policy control

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:26.650
and you have a rather
complex security environment.

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:29.050
And even if you have
the right information,

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:32.150
it's fairly difficult to know if
you've made the right decision.

00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:35.080
So as a result, in this
environment, maintaining least

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:38.200
privilege is often a challenging
and never ending task,

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:41.700
but failure can have
pretty high consequences.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.810
So at Google, we ran a
study of our customers

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:47.750
and found that 95% of
permissions that are given

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:49.310
were never used.

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:52.250
So over-provisioning is
a real common problem,

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:54.830
and a threat when
somebody you think

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:57.860
should just have read
access also has the ability

00:01:57.860 --> 00:01:59.800
to take down production.

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:01.960
And there are several
factors causing this.

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:05.200
There's a lack of visibility to
understanding how well policies

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:08.930
are configured, understanding
effective access effectively,

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:11.390
weak governance and oversight
that leads to practices

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:13.640
like model after
that go unchecked,

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.380
and the elastic
nature of Cloud that

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:17.210
makes it more
difficult to audit,

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:18.950
often leading you
to carry over access

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:21.950
from previous assignments.

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:25.070
Now, ideally, you want to
construct your policies

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:29.930
in a way that you reach a state
of measurable least privilege,

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:32.930
where you limit the attack
surface by granting users

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:36.146
only the permissions they
need at the time they need it.

00:02:36.146 --> 00:02:38.160
But we've seen that
customers often

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:40.500
hesitate about changing
their policies because they

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:41.940
don't want to break anything.

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:45.840
But this exposes them to
risk, and generally, people

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:48.060
tend to underestimate
the risk they're in.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.480
I think it's just human nature.

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:52.300
So our goal here
in this entire area

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:54.700
is to give you a better
understanding of the policies

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:57.710
you have and to help you
manage them at scale,

00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:00.400
so you can balance the trade-off
between security and developer

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:01.990
velocity.

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:03.640
After all, our
customers come to us,

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:05.230
tell us that the
promise of Cloud

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:07.510
is that we shall make
your security better,

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:08.800
just like ours.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:10.780
So we want to help
you understand

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:13.540
where you are exposed and
how you can design better

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:16.510
so you don't get breached.

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:19.060
So if you are a security
professional concerned

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:21.630
about this, like
Steve here, here

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:24.070
are some practical and
prescriptive guidance

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:26.195
from Google on how you
should think about achieving

00:03:26.195 --> 00:03:28.790
for least privilege.

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:30.900
So Steve needs to
ensure that access

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:35.230
is appropriate to business need,
and reach a goal at the end

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:38.210
where policy
management is scalable,

00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:40.210
it's timely, and effective.

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:43.860
So let's look at some of Steve's
challenges and blind spots.

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:48.120
First, how does Steve define
what is appropriate access?

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:50.460
To begin with, he needs to
know what users have access

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:52.110
to today.

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:56.220
Then, to scope it down,
what's safe to remove?

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:58.770
Steve could do this
through trial and error,

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:00.840
but that's time-consuming.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:04.300
And how do you know what
users will need in the future?

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:07.380
Secondly, how do you get a
good understanding of risk

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:10.380
without being an expert in
Google Cloud, of course.

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:12.360
Steve is concerned about
buckets being exposed

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:16.140
to the internet or the risk of
a service that can be misused

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:17.963
for lateral movement.

00:04:17.963 --> 00:04:19.380
Over-accounting
can also be caused

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:22.620
by simple, unintentional
human error that can also

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:25.230
result in data loss or
infrastructure changes that

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:27.870
are often hard to revert.

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:30.420
Now, after you solve
all these problems,

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:33.450
how do you change your
policies in a way that is safe

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:35.890
so it doesn't break
anything in production,

00:04:35.890 --> 00:04:38.700
and that scales, so
that Steve, and you,

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:42.360
can do this uniformly across
the organization using code?

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:45.850
And lastly, Steve needs to make
sure that this is measurable

00:04:45.850 --> 00:04:48.660
so he can report back that
he is moving the organization

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:50.140
towards least privilege.

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:54.320
Now, all of this is obviously
much easier said than done.

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:58.380
At Google, we believe
that solving this problem

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:00.270
by just hiring and
staffing cybersecurity

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:03.000
experts in today's world
is not a scalable solution

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:04.540
to these problems.

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:08.130
We believe the answer lies in
intelligence and automation.

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:11.610
And so we built our product,
Policy Intelligence,

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:15.210
to assist users as they move
through the lifecycle of policy

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:19.890
management to manage this policy
securely and with confidence.

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:22.120
And we do this by fusing
the magic of Google,

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:25.000
which is data, machine
learning, throughout your Cloud

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:27.280
experience.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:29.980
A way to think about this
is think about the way cars

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:31.150
have evolved.

00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:33.970
Today's cars kind of seem
like the car on the left.

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:36.490
It's manual and unguided.

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:39.400
And we want to upgrade the
experience like modern cars

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:42.010
and beyond with
the help of tools

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:45.410
that we provide to make
your security much better.

00:05:45.410 --> 00:05:47.590
So we want to effectively
take policy management

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:50.560
through this same upgrade
to achieve intelligence

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:53.560
and automation, like
we did with cars.

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:55.570
So we asked
ourselves, what can we

00:05:55.570 --> 00:05:58.240
do to empower our
customers in this way?

00:05:58.240 --> 00:05:59.740
And after speaking
with many of you,

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:03.540
we found that the answers
were clearly in three areas.

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:07.650
First, you want to find
out what went wrong quickly

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:11.700
so you take action right away
with troubleshooting tools.

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:13.920
Secondly, you want
to prevent mistakes

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:17.360
from happening altogether
with analytical tools.

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:19.950
And thirdly, you want to
improve your Cloud easily

00:06:19.950 --> 00:06:22.050
with actionable recommendations.

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:24.330
Now to talk to you and
walk through these products

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:28.220
in details, I'll hand it over
to Vandy for a deep dive.

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:29.790
VANDHANA RAMADURA: Thanks, Abhi.

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:32.630
So let's start with
Policy Troubleshooter.

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:36.560
We designed it to make it super
easy for you to understand

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:39.530
why a user doesn't have access.

00:06:39.530 --> 00:06:41.780
When you're in the midst
of doing something,

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:44.030
it's frustrating to
see an error message

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:46.940
and not know what to do
next, especially when

00:06:46.940 --> 00:06:50.630
you expect your policies
have been set up correctly.

00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:53.070
This might seem
like a simple issue,

00:06:53.070 --> 00:06:55.840
but it can be surprisingly
hard to troubleshoot.

00:06:55.840 --> 00:06:58.070
Without Troubleshooter,
you'd have

00:06:58.070 --> 00:07:03.140
to scan all the policies across
the hierarchy one by one.

00:07:03.140 --> 00:07:06.830
You'd have to find the policy
that allows the API call.

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:09.020
And if that policy
has a group, you'd

00:07:09.020 --> 00:07:12.390
need to manually check if
the user is in the group.

00:07:12.390 --> 00:07:16.280
All of this takes a lot
of time, and our customers

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:19.680
don't want security to be
in the way of productivity.

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:21.890
So admins like
Steve would usually

00:07:21.890 --> 00:07:24.440
unblock their developers
by granting them

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:28.460
overly permissioned roles
just so they're not blocked.

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:32.630
Now, with self-service tools,
like Policy Troubleshooter,

00:07:32.630 --> 00:07:36.410
a developer can hit Troubleshoot
when they see an error message

00:07:36.410 --> 00:07:40.280
and share the URL of the
Troubleshooter to Steve.

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:44.870
The error message automatically
passes the principle, resource,

00:07:44.870 --> 00:07:47.570
and permission.

00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:51.140
Policy Troubleshooter will show
you all the relevant policies

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:54.230
across the hierarchy
so admins like Steve

00:07:54.230 --> 00:07:56.510
can make a decision
on the best place

00:07:56.510 --> 00:07:59.360
to grant the developer access.

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:01.850
It helps Steve
understand which roles

00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:04.520
are needed to unblock
this developer,

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:06.680
and also helps
you make decisions

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:10.250
on where in the
hierarchy to grant roles.

00:08:10.250 --> 00:08:13.670
At times, a user might even
be blocked because they're not

00:08:13.670 --> 00:08:16.190
a member of the right
group, and Troubleshooter

00:08:16.190 --> 00:08:19.280
can help you figure this out.

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:23.030
Troubleshooter also highlights
dynamic access control issues,

00:08:23.030 --> 00:08:25.320
such as when
conditions are applied.

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:28.910
For example, a developer
might get an error

00:08:28.910 --> 00:08:31.960
if she access as a
resource after 5:00 PM,

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:33.890
and Troubleshooter
will easily tell

00:08:33.890 --> 00:08:38.659
Steve the issue is the user did
not meet the time condition.

00:08:38.659 --> 00:08:40.940
With Policy
Troubleshooter, Steve now

00:08:40.940 --> 00:08:44.360
feels that he's in a position
to not only establish

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:46.340
the principle of
least privilege,

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:50.000
but actually maintain it instead
of simply granting overly

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:52.610
permissive roles to
everyone who comes

00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:57.920
to him with "my access isn't
working" type of issues.

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:01.660
So here are some ways you can
use Policy Troubleshooter.

00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:03.790
If you're not sure if
a service account that

00:09:03.790 --> 00:09:06.670
is buried in a group
can reach a resource,

00:09:06.670 --> 00:09:09.220
Troubleshooter will show
you group membership

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:11.440
even for nested groups.

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:14.140
Designed as an admin
self-service tool,

00:09:14.140 --> 00:09:18.160
you can integrate this into
your helpdesk tools like Jira.

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:20.440
And we're excited
to announce we're

00:09:20.440 --> 00:09:25.040
extending this to support VPC
Service Controls and Identity

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:28.400
Aware Proxy errors.

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:31.250
While Steve was troubleshooting
for his developer,

00:09:31.250 --> 00:09:35.510
he was surprised to see owner
roles assigned on his project.

00:09:35.510 --> 00:09:38.810
So Policy Analyzer
is our query tool

00:09:38.810 --> 00:09:42.050
that can help you reduce
your organizational risk

00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:44.690
through improved visibility.

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:46.670
If you're looking
to understand who

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:50.840
has access to what, what
does that access secure,

00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:53.640
and what are users
doing with that access,

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:57.890
you can do so efficiently
with Policy Analyzer.

00:09:57.890 --> 00:10:00.410
Steve quickly uses
Policy Analyzer

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:03.920
to find out who has the owner
role in his organization

00:10:03.920 --> 00:10:05.990
and where it was granted.

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:10.100
He selects the owner
role and does the query.

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:14.270
In the results, Steve can now
see members who have this role.

00:10:14.270 --> 00:10:18.440
With Policy Analyzer, Steve is
able to answer this question

00:10:18.440 --> 00:10:20.030
and many more.

00:10:20.030 --> 00:10:22.590
In addition to this
one he had in mind,

00:10:22.590 --> 00:10:25.310
he runs a couple
of other queries.

00:10:25.310 --> 00:10:27.470
Which VMs can John delete?

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:30.920
Who has write access on
this BigQuery data set?

00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:31.940
It feels like magic.

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:37.310
If you're wondering how
someone's access will change

00:10:37.310 --> 00:10:39.740
when they move from
one group to another,

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:44.420
use Policy Analyzer to
understand access impact.

00:10:44.420 --> 00:10:47.390
Also use it to query
and create reports

00:10:47.390 --> 00:10:51.860
for privilege escalation
and access certification.

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:55.130
And if you're wondering what
changed in a policy and when,

00:10:55.130 --> 00:10:57.800
use Policy Analyzer
to understand

00:10:57.800 --> 00:11:00.896
the history of your policies.

00:11:00.896 --> 00:11:03.640
But before Steve
changes the order role,

00:11:03.640 --> 00:11:06.670
he wants to make sure he
doesn't break anything.

00:11:06.670 --> 00:11:10.420
With Policy Simulator, Steve
can check the proposed policy

00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:14.740
changes against the 90-day
access history of the user

00:11:14.740 --> 00:11:18.490
to see if the changes would
block any essential access that

00:11:18.490 --> 00:11:20.320
was previously used.

00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:23.920
Steve knows Anita often
works in BigQuery.

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:29.180
So he changes Anita's owner
rule to BigQuery admin.

00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:32.300
Policy Simulator shows
which permissions

00:11:32.300 --> 00:11:35.210
would have been [? usually ?]
granted with a BigQuery admin

00:11:35.210 --> 00:11:38.840
role, and which permissions
would be revoked.

00:11:38.840 --> 00:11:41.360
With Policy Simulator's
results, Steve

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:45.860
can the impact of the change and
ensure Anita's productivity is

00:11:45.860 --> 00:11:48.910
not impacted.

00:11:48.910 --> 00:11:52.900
These analytical tools, like
Troubleshooter, Analyzer,

00:11:52.900 --> 00:11:55.540
and Simulator, can
help you figure out

00:11:55.540 --> 00:11:58.330
what your current
state policies are.

00:11:58.330 --> 00:12:00.820
Then we have tools,
like the Recommender,

00:12:00.820 --> 00:12:02.920
to give you proactive
guidance on how

00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:05.020
to achieve these privilege.

00:12:05.020 --> 00:12:09.652
And to tell you about this,
I'll hand it back to Abhi.

00:12:09.652 --> 00:12:10.860
ABHI YADAV: Thank you, Vandy.

00:12:10.860 --> 00:12:12.180
That was great.

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:16.080
So we introduced IAM Recommender
last year at Next 2019

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:19.360
to help customers achieve least
privilege with least effort.

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:21.690
And since then, we are
super excited that thousands

00:12:21.690 --> 00:12:24.240
of customers around the world
are using this every day

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:26.500
to minimize their
attack surface.

00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:29.880
Now, this product helps security
and nonsecurity professionals

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:33.000
identify unneeded access to GCP.

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:35.940
Using machine learning, we
provide smart access control

00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:37.320
recommendations.

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:40.140
Right here in the IAM
page, the Recommender

00:12:40.140 --> 00:12:42.870
is going to show you how
many permissions are given

00:12:42.870 --> 00:12:44.940
and how many are
actually being used.

00:12:44.940 --> 00:12:48.300
There's no need to collect and
pass through activity loads.

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:50.550
We've done all that
hard work for you.

00:12:50.550 --> 00:12:53.730
In the back end, we store and
process petabytes of log data

00:12:53.730 --> 00:12:57.180
daily at no additional
charge to our customers.

00:12:57.180 --> 00:12:59.760
We now simply show you
this data and these results

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:02.580
right here next to the
user's role assignment.

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:05.040
Now, it looks like most of
the members in my project

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:06.240
here are OK.

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:09.330
There are a few that are using
a lot less than were granted.

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:11.760
And you can see three
light bulbs have popped up.

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:13.770
This is where our
ML models have found

00:13:13.770 --> 00:13:16.470
a recommendation for
a better fitting goal

00:13:16.470 --> 00:13:18.450
automatically, in
the back ground.

00:13:18.450 --> 00:13:20.470
I had to do no work.

00:13:20.470 --> 00:13:22.570
Now, this service
account on the top row

00:13:22.570 --> 00:13:25.860
has owner role with about
1,800 permissions assigned,

00:13:25.860 --> 00:13:28.730
but it has used only 31 of them.

00:13:28.730 --> 00:13:30.790
So now, IAM Recommender
is going to tell me

00:13:30.790 --> 00:13:33.970
that I should change its role
to reduce the attacks efforts.

00:13:33.970 --> 00:13:38.150
And if I need, I can
click to get more details.

00:13:38.150 --> 00:13:40.030
So here the tool
shows me the analysis

00:13:40.030 --> 00:13:41.770
behind the recommendation.

00:13:41.770 --> 00:13:45.220
It suggests the service account
should be moved to the BigQuery

00:13:45.220 --> 00:13:47.740
admin role and why
by highlighting

00:13:47.740 --> 00:13:50.980
the permissions here that
are safe to remove in red.

00:13:50.980 --> 00:13:52.840
ML also picks out
that this account

00:13:52.840 --> 00:13:54.760
would need the
saved queries update

00:13:54.760 --> 00:13:59.250
permission in the future based
on similar usage patterns.

00:13:59.250 --> 00:14:01.540
Now, this is a permission
it already has,

00:14:01.540 --> 00:14:03.340
but that it has not yet used.

00:14:03.340 --> 00:14:07.580
And you can find this marked
anywhere with the blue ML icon.

00:14:07.580 --> 00:14:10.480
So we do this because we want to
make sure that users or service

00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:12.550
accounts don't miss
out on permissions

00:14:12.550 --> 00:14:16.320
and don't have to come
back later to request more.

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:18.200
And now, in our
latest update, we

00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:20.120
have also added
the ability for you

00:14:20.120 --> 00:14:22.500
to customize the recommendation.

00:14:22.500 --> 00:14:25.100
If you want to remove a
permission or add one,

00:14:25.100 --> 00:14:28.460
you can do that right here
in the recommendations screen

00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:31.610
in context, which
creates a custom role.

00:14:31.610 --> 00:14:33.080
And now your custom
role would be

00:14:33.080 --> 00:14:37.700
based on actual activity data
and improved with Google ML

00:14:37.700 --> 00:14:41.240
to suggest which permissions you
need to add based on observed

00:14:41.240 --> 00:14:43.040
patterns in the Cloud.

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:46.640
So you just need to hit apply,
and that would make the change,

00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:48.590
creates a custom
role, and assigns it

00:14:48.590 --> 00:14:50.003
to the service account.

00:14:50.003 --> 00:14:51.920
And now, this custom
role would be recommended

00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:53.150
to other members as needed.

00:14:56.210 --> 00:14:58.790
Another area we have
invested in this year

00:14:58.790 --> 00:15:02.310
is making recommendations
easier to discover.

00:15:02.310 --> 00:15:04.910
So project teams can
self-serve in the Cloud,

00:15:04.910 --> 00:15:07.240
in the Cloud console
in the IAM page,

00:15:07.240 --> 00:15:08.760
as we have shown you already.

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:10.810
Now, they can also
find recommendations

00:15:10.810 --> 00:15:14.100
in our recommendation hub that
is located in the Cloud console

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:15.430
dashboard.

00:15:15.430 --> 00:15:18.250
Here, project teams can
give you the right insights

00:15:18.250 --> 00:15:20.230
and the recommendations
and apply them

00:15:20.230 --> 00:15:22.260
if they have the permission.

00:15:22.260 --> 00:15:24.650
Secondly, for central
security teams,

00:15:24.650 --> 00:15:28.430
we now support out of the box
integration with BigQuery.

00:15:28.430 --> 00:15:30.130
So with a few simple
steps, you can

00:15:30.130 --> 00:15:33.490
export all your
recommendations to a VQ table.

00:15:33.490 --> 00:15:35.740
Now this will allow you to
create all the right custom

00:15:35.740 --> 00:15:36.700
reports.

00:15:36.700 --> 00:15:40.090
So for example, if you want
a view of over-provisioning

00:15:40.090 --> 00:15:42.010
across your
production workloads,

00:15:42.010 --> 00:15:44.800
or if you want to analyze a
given permission for a service

00:15:44.800 --> 00:15:47.640
account, you can do that
very easily here using

00:15:47.640 --> 00:15:50.110
our analytical tools.

00:15:50.110 --> 00:15:54.070
And lastly, we expose
insights and recommendations

00:15:54.070 --> 00:15:57.100
through APIs with methods
such as get, clean,

00:15:57.100 --> 00:15:58.570
mark as succeeded.

00:15:58.570 --> 00:16:01.790
So in addition to
automating recommendations,

00:16:01.790 --> 00:16:03.700
we've also have some
customers use this

00:16:03.700 --> 00:16:06.070
to create alerts and reminders.

00:16:06.070 --> 00:16:07.810
So maybe they are
sending weekly digest

00:16:07.810 --> 00:16:09.820
to the project
teams alerting them

00:16:09.820 --> 00:16:12.943
that they should take action.

00:16:12.943 --> 00:16:14.860
So here are some ways
that you can get started

00:16:14.860 --> 00:16:16.990
with IAM Recommender today.

00:16:16.990 --> 00:16:20.320
Number one, to protect
against lateral movement,

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:23.270
we recommend a defense
in depth strategy.

00:16:23.270 --> 00:16:25.450
A key piece of this
approach includes

00:16:25.450 --> 00:16:29.200
limiting members who have the
act ask permission on projects,

00:16:29.200 --> 00:16:31.310
as well as service accounts.

00:16:31.310 --> 00:16:35.320
So you can use IAM Recommender
to ensure that users are not

00:16:35.320 --> 00:16:38.950
assigned a service account
user role at the project level.

00:16:38.950 --> 00:16:41.080
Also, you can use
the Recommender

00:16:41.080 --> 00:16:44.080
to understand where service
accounts have permissions

00:16:44.080 --> 00:16:45.710
across projects.

00:16:45.710 --> 00:16:48.970
So for example, if a service
account that is in project A

00:16:48.970 --> 00:16:51.010
has been granted a
role in Project B,

00:16:51.010 --> 00:16:52.750
you can use Recommender
to understand

00:16:52.750 --> 00:16:55.810
where these can be removed
to mitigate lateral movement

00:16:55.810 --> 00:16:57.430
threats.

00:16:57.430 --> 00:17:01.370
Secondly, just don't use
owner and editor legacy roles.

00:17:01.370 --> 00:17:02.690
These are not recommended.

00:17:02.690 --> 00:17:04.960
And you can use IAM
Recommender today

00:17:04.960 --> 00:17:08.130
to scope down the access
to more granular roles.

00:17:08.130 --> 00:17:10.810
Our product now provides
a migration path

00:17:10.810 --> 00:17:14.800
from these legacy roles to
multiple and smaller roles,

00:17:14.800 --> 00:17:16.109
which is best practice.

00:17:16.109 --> 00:17:18.069
So you can go from
a large owner role

00:17:18.069 --> 00:17:20.430
to more fine grained roles.

00:17:20.430 --> 00:17:22.180
And lastly, as you
saw earlier, you

00:17:22.180 --> 00:17:25.400
can use this feature to create
meaningful custom roles based

00:17:25.400 --> 00:17:28.550
on activity data, actual
activity data, and machine

00:17:28.550 --> 00:17:30.460
learning.

00:17:30.460 --> 00:17:33.430
And lastly, I'd like to
share with you some pro tips.

00:17:33.430 --> 00:17:35.590
If you have, like many
of your customers,

00:17:35.590 --> 00:17:37.950
thousands of projects
and recommendations,

00:17:37.950 --> 00:17:40.780
do start with developing
a prioritization strategy

00:17:40.780 --> 00:17:43.042
and a framework to
identify the severity

00:17:43.042 --> 00:17:45.250
of these recommendations in
the context of your Cloud

00:17:45.250 --> 00:17:47.230
environment so you
can understand which

00:17:47.230 --> 00:17:49.870
ones you want to apply first.

00:17:49.870 --> 00:17:51.940
Think about exporting
these recommendations

00:17:51.940 --> 00:17:55.420
to your existing IGA or
access certification tools,

00:17:55.420 --> 00:17:58.300
and this will help speed up your
resource owner or user manager

00:17:58.300 --> 00:18:00.650
certifications.

00:18:00.650 --> 00:18:03.800
And you can use these
recommendations with insights

00:18:03.800 --> 00:18:08.330
to understand what access
a user has across projects.

00:18:08.330 --> 00:18:10.130
And lastly, when
using our APIs, don't

00:18:10.130 --> 00:18:13.740
forget to mark recommendations
as succeeded, as when you have

00:18:13.740 --> 00:18:16.150
applied them, this will
help you track your progress

00:18:16.150 --> 00:18:18.830
towards least privilege.

